// =============================================================================
// Agriculture ERP -- Prisma schema
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RLS AND THIS CONNECTION -- DO NOT "FIX" THIS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Prisma connects as the database owner and therefore BYPASSES row-level
// security. That is correct and intended.
//
// RLS is our backstop against the anon and authenticated Supabase keys, which
// reach the database directly from clients. It is NOT our defence against our
// own server. Our server-side control is requireRole in the Next.js API
// routes, which verifies session and role before any query runs.
//
// So: every table enables RLS with no policies (deny-by-default), and Prisma
// bypasses it as owner. Both are deliberate. A future session that "fixes"
// this by connecting Prisma as a restricted role will break every API route
// and gain nothing.
//
// Prisma cannot author RLS policies. There is no schema syntax for ENABLE ROW
// LEVEL SECURITY or CREATE POLICY in any Prisma version. Policies are
// hand-written SQL in migrations, which is why the rule lives as a comment in
// the migration files themselves.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MIGRATIONS -- `prisma migrate dev` IS NOT USED ON THIS PROJECT
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `migrate dev` wants to create a shadow database to diff against. The
// Supabase `postgres` role cannot create databases, so it times out or fails.
// That failure is expected and is not a bug to chase.
//
// Migrations here are hand-written SQL in timestamped folders under
// prisma/migrations, applied with `prisma migrate deploy` (pnpm db:migrate).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE THE CONNECTION URLS LIVE -- NOT IN THIS FILE
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Prisma 7 removed `url` and `directUrl` from the datasource block. They are
// no longer valid here and the schema will not parse if you add them back.
//
//   DIRECT_URL    Direct connection, port 5432. Read in prisma.config.ts as
//                 `datasource.url`. This is what MIGRATIONS use. Migrations
//                 cannot run through the pooler.
//
//   DATABASE_URL  Pooled connection, port 6543, with pgbouncer=true and
//                 connection_limit=1. Passed to the PrismaPg driver adapter
//                 when a PrismaClient is constructed. This is what the
//                 APPLICATION uses at runtime.
//
// Both carry ?schema=public, which is how Prisma is restricted to the public
// schema and never touches the auth, storage, realtime or graphql schemas
// that Supabase manages itself.
//
// Real values live in .env.local only, never in this repository.
// =============================================================================

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

/// Throwaway smoke-test table for unit B1.3. Proves a migration applies and
/// reverses. Created in migration 2, dropped in migration 3. Holds no real
/// data and is not part of the data model.
model Smoke {
  id   Int    @id @default(autoincrement())
  note String

  @@map("_smoke")
}
